solution.text:
Given, the heat of neutralisation of a strong base and a strong acid is $13.7 \mathrm{kcal}$.
The reaction of neutralisation is as follows
$$
\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{HCl}+\mathrm{NaOH} \longrightarrow \mathrm{NaCl}+\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O} = -137kcal \\
1 \mathrm{mol} \quad 1 \mathrm{mol} 
\end{array}
$$
According to question,
$$
\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{HCl}+\mathrm{NaOH} \longrightarrow \mathrm{NaCl}+\mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O} \\
0.6 \mathrm{mol} \quad 0.25 \mathrm{mol}
\end{array}
$$
In equation (i), NaOH acts as a limiting reagent. For 1 mole of NaOH and 1 mole of HCl, heat of neutralisation $=137 \mathrm{kcal}$
: For 0.25 mole of NaOH and 0.6 mole of $\mathrm{HCl}$, heat of neutralisation $=137 \times 0.25 \Rightarrow 3.425 \mathrm{kcal}$
